@comment{
====================================================================
DOMAIN: Primary Texts, Textual History, and Systematic Location
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-09-04
PAPERS_FOUND: 14 total (High: 9, Medium: 5, Low: 0)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, IEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, CORE, CrossRef,
  NDPR. OpenAlex returned persistent 429 rate-limit errors during bulk
  domain searches in this session; abstract enrichment (get_abstract.py)
  nonetheless reached OpenAlex successfully on several individual DOI
  look-ups, so it was not completely unavailable, just unreliable for
  bulk queries.

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
Hegel never published a book called "Philosophy of History." What
readers encounter under that title is a posthumous editorial
construction. The starting point is the 1837 Vorlesungen über die
Philosophie der Geschichte, assembled by Eduard Gans (and later revised
by Karl Hegel) from Hegel's own fragmentary manuscript for the
Introduction plus multiple sets of student transcripts (Nachschriften)
of the lecture courses Hegel gave in Berlin in 1822/23, 1824/25,
1826/27, and 1830/31. Because no single authorial manuscript for the
lecture series survives, every printed "Philosophy of History" is an
editorial hybrid, and the three major moments in the editorial
tradition — Gans/Karl Hegel's Werke edition (the basis of Sibree's
1857 English translation), Johannes Hoffmeister's 1955 edition of the
Introduction alone (translated by Nisbet as "Reason in History," with
Duncan Forbes's introduction), and Karl-Heinz Ilting's six-volume
critical edition of the parallel lecture complex on Rechtsphilosophie —
make materially different editorial choices about how to weight
Hegel's own manuscript against the transcripts, and about which
transcript(s) to privilege where they diverge. Hodgson and Brown's
Oxford edition of the Lectures on the Philosophy of World History
(Volume I, 2011; Volume II, 2025) is the most recent attempt to resolve
these problems by translating specific dated lecture courses (1822-23
and 1830-31) against the German critical edition (Jaeschke et al.,
Hegel: Gesammelte Werke) rather than the composite Werke text, with an
apparatus recording variants across the surviving transcripts. Jaeschke
and Bauer's overview of the Gesammelte Werke project (the Hegel-Archiv
edition housed at Bochum) explains the rationale and scope of this
ongoing critical-edition effort, of which the lecture volumes are one
part.

The domain's second cluster of issues concerns systematic location:
where does "history" belong in Hegel's architecture? History is not a
free-standing topic for Hegel; it appears at the seam between
Objective Spirit (Recht, Moralität, Sittlichkeit — the Philosophy of
Right, culminating in §§341-360 on world history as the "world's
court of judgment") and Absolute Spirit (art, religion, philosophy),
and it is also treated compactly in the Encyclopedia's transition
passage (§§548-552). Kervégan's systematic study of Objective Spirit,
Oehl's account of how "spirit's self-knowledge" requires history as
the hinge into the Absolute, and Alznauer's discussion of Bykova's
edited guide to the Encyclopedia Philosophy of Spirit all address this
placement question, as does the recurring worry (voiced by Rojek,
Sedgwick, and Yang/Yeomans in different ways) that "world history"
draws on categories — necessity, teleology, syllogism (Schluss) —
developed abstractly in the Science of Logic, so that the philosophy
of history cannot be read independently of Hegel's logical categories
of teleological mediation.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
Any comprehensive review of Hegel's philosophy of history must first
establish which text is being discussed and on what editorial
authority, since substantive debates elsewhere in the literature (e.g.
about the "end of history," about Eurocentrism, about the
necessity/contingency of historical development) often turn on
readings of specific lecture-course passages whose wording and
even presence varies across editions. This domain also supplies the
systematic vocabulary (Objective/Absolute Spirit, the Logic's
teleology) that other domains in this review will presuppose.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
English-language secondary literature specifically on the comparative
editorial politics of Hoffmeister vs. Ilting vs. the older Werke
tradition is thin outside German-language Hegel-Studien scholarship;
Grossmann (1996) is the most directly relevant piece found, but no
abstract could be retrieved for it (flagged INCOMPLETE below), and the
Ilting edition itself (1973) is likewise flagged INCOMPLETE for lack of
a retrievable abstract. No secondary literature was found specifically
on Encyclopedia §§548-552 in isolation; discussion of that transition
is instead absorbed into broader treatments of Objective/Absolute
Spirit (Kervégan, Oehl, Alznauer/Bykova). SEP/IEP citation-context
extraction (get_sep_context.py) returned no in-text matches for any
paper in this domain's final list, so no sep_context/iep_context
fields could be populated.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Open the review by establishing the textual/editorial situation before
any thematic discussion, since it materially affects how much weight
individual passages can bear. Use Kervégan and Oehl to frame the
Objective Spirit/Absolute Spirit transition, and Sedgwick/Yang &
Yeomans/Rojek to introduce the Logic-derived vocabulary (necessity,
teleology) that recurs across the rest of the review.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Editorial/textual scholarship: 5 items (Hodgson & Brown 2025; Hegel &
  Forbes/Nisbet 1975; Ilting 1973; Jaeschke & Bauer 2014; Grossmann
  1996) - document the composite, transcript-based character of the
  "Philosophy of History" text and the successive critical editions.
- Systematic location (Objective/Absolute Spirit): 4 items (Kervégan
  2018; Oehl 2018; Alznauer 2021; De Laurentiis 2005) - address where
  world history and the history of philosophy sit within Hegel's
  architecture.
- Logic-derived categories (necessity, teleology): 3 items (Sedgwick
  2016; Yang & Yeomans 2023; Rojek 2017) - trace how Science of Logic
  categories structure the philosophy of history.
- Overview/guidebook and reception: 2 items (McCarney 2012; Pinkard
  2025) - synthetic framings of the field as a whole.
====================================================================
}

@book{hodgsonbrown2025lectures,
  author = {Hodgson, Peter C. and Brown, Robert F.},
  title = {Hegel: Lectures on the Philosophy of World History, Volume II},
  year = {2025},
  publisher = {Oxford University Press},
  doi = {10.1093/9780198943273.001.0001},
  abstract = {The first volume, containing the Manuscripts of the Introduction and the Lectures of 1822–23, was published by OUP in 2011. The second volume, containing the last of Hegel's lectures on this topic, had to await the German critical edition of the Lectures of 1830–31, which appeared in 2020. The main source is the transcription by Karl Hegel (the philosopher's son), but three other transcriptions of these lectures have been examined, and variations and additions from these other sources are included in an elaborate apparatus. A simplified and abbreviated version of the apparatus appears in translation. The manuscripts of the introduction contained in volume 1 include Hegel's own incomplete lecture manuscript for the Introduction to the Lectures in 1830–31. The transcription of the lectures as delivered provides an interesting comparison with what Hegel actually wrote. The present volume contains a lengthy Introduction that includes a discussion of the nature of Spirit, the concrete existence of freedom, the means Spirit uses, the state and ethical life, erroneous views of freedom and the state, constitution or systems of government, the course of world history, and the usefulness of world history. A preliminary part includes sections on climate and geography, the distinction between the old world and the new world, how the course of world history moves from East to West, and the four main divisions of it (Oriental, Greek, Roman, and European). While the German editors have provided complete critical editions of the lectures for every year that Hegel delivered them, only the first and last years appear in our English edition.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: This is the second (and concluding) volume of the Oxford critical translation of Hegel's lecture courses on world history, covering the 1830-31 lectures. Its main source is Karl Hegel's transcription, checked against three other student transcripts, with variants recorded in an apparatus; this had to await the 2020 German critical edition of the 1830-31 course. The editors note that only the first (1822-23) and last (1830-31) of the years Hegel lectured on this material are translated in full into English, even though the German critical edition covers every year.

  RELEVANCE: Directly documents the manuscript/transcript problem the domain foregrounds: no single authorial text exists, only Hegel's incomplete introduction manuscript plus multiple, sometimes divergent, Nachschriften. Establishes that current English-language scholarship on the "late" Hegel's philosophy of history rests on a translation that is itself an edited selection from a larger German critical apparatus.

  POSITION: Represents the most recent stage of the editorial tradition, superseding composite 19th-century texts by anchoring translation to dated, individually attested lecture courses.
  },
  keywords = {textual-history, editions, lectures-on-world-history, High}
}

@book{hegelforbes1975lectures,
  author = {Hegel, Georg Wilhelm Friedrich and Forbes, Duncan},
  title = {Lectures on the Philosophy of World History},
  year = {1975},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  doi = {10.1017/cbo9781139167567},
  abstract = {An English translation of Hegel's introduction to his lectures on the philosophy of history, based directly on the standard German edition by Johannes Hoffmeister, first published in 1955. The previous English translation, by J. Sibree, first appeared in 1857 and was based on the defective German edition of Karl Hegel, to which Hoffmeister's edition added a large amount of new material previously unknown to English readers, derived from earlier editors. In the introduction to his lectures, Hegel lays down the principles and aims which underlie his philosophy of history, and provides an outline of the philosophy of history itself. The comprehensive and voluminous survey of world history which followed the introduction in the original lectures is of less interest to students of Hegel's thought than the introduction, and is therefore not included in this volume.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: This Cambridge edition translates (by H. B. Nisbet, with an introduction by Duncan Forbes) the Introduction to Hegel's lectures on world history on the basis of Johannes Hoffmeister's 1955 German edition, itself a reworking of the posthumous 1837 Vorlesungen über die Philosophie der Geschichte. Per its own abstract, Hoffmeister's edition added substantial material unavailable to Sibree, whose 1857 translation relied on Karl Hegel's "defective" Werke text.

  RELEVANCE: Represents the mid-20th-century stage of the editorial tradition, standing between the earlier Gans/Karl Hegel Werke text used for Sibree's translation and the later Hodgson/Brown edition based on the 2020 German critical edition. The explicit contrast the abstract draws with Sibree's translation is precisely the kind of editorial divergence this domain's key questions ask about.

  POSITION: The Hoffmeister/Nisbet edition in the chain from Gans/Karl Hegel (Werke) to Hoffmeister to the Akademie/Gesammelte-Werke-based Hodgson-Brown translation.
  },
  keywords = {textual-history, editions, hoffmeister, reason-in-history, High}
}

